# Normalize to LF in the repository *and* in every working tree. GitHub's
# Windows runners ship git with `core.autocrlf=true`, so without this the
# Windows leg of the CI matrix tests a checkout artefact rather than the code,
# and a fragment committed from Windows would ship CRLF inside the published
# archive.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Splicer goldens encode their line endings deliberately -- one fixture pair
# exists specifically to prove CRLF survives a splice. Git must not normalize
# them, or the Windows leg of the CI matrix fails on a checkout artefact.
# `!eol` is not redundant: it makes the eol setting *unspecified* for these
# paths rather than leaving the `*` rule's `lf` visible on them.
test/fixtures/** -text !eol
